**Clarify the requirement.** Does "1000 at a time" mean the client requests page after page, or the server streams everything in 1000-row batches? I read it as: the server reads from the database in batches of 1000 and writes them to the response as a byte stream, so the whole result is never in memory.

**Approach.**
- **Read in pages.** Use **keyset pagination** (`WHERE id > :lastId ORDER BY id LIMIT 1000`), which stays fast as the offset grows; `OFFSET` pagination gets slower the deeper you go. Alternatively use a JDBC streaming cursor with `fetchSize` set (driver-specific: PostgreSQL needs auto-commit off, MySQL needs a streaming flag).
- **Write to an output stream** instead of returning a `List`. In Spring MVC, return `StreamingResponseBody`:

```java
@GetMapping("/export")
public ResponseEntity<StreamingResponseBody> export() {
    StreamingResponseBody body = out -> {
        long last = 0; List<Rec> page;
        do {
            page = repo.findPage(last, 1000);
            for (Rec r : page) out.write((toJson(r) + "\n").getBytes(UTF_8));
            out.flush();
            if (!page.isEmpty()) last = page.get(page.size() - 1).getId();
        } while (page.size() == 1000);
    };
    return ResponseEntity.ok().contentType(MediaType.APPLICATION_NDJSON).body(body);
}
```
Newline-delimited JSON or CSV is easy to consume incrementally. Memory stays at one page. Handle client disconnects and set timeouts. If the client wants interactive pages, expose a `cursor` token rather than a page number.

**Other topics, briefly.**
- **Idempotency:** repeating a request has the same effect as doing it once. `GET`, `PUT`, `DELETE` are idempotent by definition; make `POST` safe using an idempotency key stored with the result.
- **ArrayList vs LinkedList:** `ArrayList` is a resizable array with O(1) index access and amortised O(1) append, inserts in the middle cost O(n); `LinkedList` has O(1) insert or remove at a known node but O(n) access, plus more memory per element. `ArrayList` is the default choice.
- **Bean scopes:** singleton (default), prototype, and in web apps request, session, application, websocket. Without `@Scope`, use the composed annotations `@RequestScope`, `@SessionScope`, `@ApplicationScope`, or define `scope` in XML configuration.
